<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anklam</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anklam is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecklenburg-vorpommern">Mecklenburg-Western Pomerania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peene">Peene</a> river near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szczecin">Szczecin</a> Lagoon. A member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a> from the late 13th century, it was Swedish from 1648 until 1720, when it passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. The aviation pioneer Otto Lilienthal was born there in 1848.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anklam.jpg" alt="Anklam" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Michael Sander · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marktplatz_Anklam.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Anklam, formerly known as Tanglim and Wendenburg, lies on the banks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peene">Peene</a> river, 8 kilometres from its mouth in the Kleines Haff, the western part of the Szczecin Lagoon. It is the head of navigation on the Peene. It was the capital of the former district of Ostvorpommern and, since September 2011, belongs to the district of Vorpommern-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greifswald">Greifswald</a>. It had 12,177 inhabitants in 2021.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> an important Scandinavian and Wendish settlement stood nearby, at the place now called Altes Lager Menzlin, and Anklam began as an associated Wendish fortress. Part of the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a>, the abandoned fortress became a settlement named Tanglim after its new founder during the German expansion eastwards. It received town status in 1244 and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>, a membership that brought it a measure of wealth, though it stayed small.</p><p>Because of its military importance Anklam suffered greatly in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>, when Swedish and Imperial troops fought over it for twenty years; Imperial forces held it from 1627 to 1630 and Swedish forces afterwards. It became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Swedish Pomerania</a> in 1648 and was captured by Frederick William of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> in 1676. In 1713 Russian soldiers looted it; the town was not burned, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a> had ordered, largely through the resistance of Christian Thomesen Carl. The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a> in 1720 gave the southern parts of Swedish Pomerania to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. In the 19th century railways joined it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szczecin">Stettin</a>, and linen, leather, beer and soap were made there.</p><p>Several bombing raids by the U.S. Air Force in 1943 and 1944, and the burning and levelling of most of the town by the advancing Soviets in the last days of the war, nearly destroyed it. In February 1945 a death march of Allied prisoners of war from Stalag XX-B passed through. After the war it was rebuilt in a rather uniform socialist style and, from 1952 to 1990, formed part of Bezirk Neubrandenburg.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Among its remaining buildings is the church of St Mary, of the 12th century, rebuilt in the 15th century, given a modern spire in the 19th and repaired in 1947. The Otto-Lilienthal-Museum commemorates the aviation pioneer, born there in 1848, and the Museum im Steintor covers local history. Others born in Anklam include the architect Gustav Lilienthal, the diplomat Ulrich von Hassell and the opera singer Johanna Gadski.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Anklam” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Power was shared with the estates and with Hanseatic towns such as Stralsund, Greifswald and <b>Anklam</b>, which grew rich on Baltic trade, and Greifswald University was founded in 1456.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-pomerania-wolgast">Duchy of Pomerania-Wolgast</a></li></ul></section>
